 "tastes": "<p>Irish dry stout is the beer most people picture when they hear the word stout, and it is lighter than it looks. Most are around 4 to 4.5 percent, with a body closer to a bitter than a strong ale. The black colour and sharp coffee bite come from roasted barley, unmalted grain roasted until almost charred, which gives a dry, slightly acrid edge that defines the style.</p><p>It grew out of London porter, which Dublin brewers adapted in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries until it became their own. Flaked barley in the grist helps the head, and many breweries pour it on nitrogen, which gives the slow cascade and the thick, creamy collar of foam.</p><p>Drink one and look for coffee without the milk, a firm but not harsh bitterness, and a quick, dry finish. Nothing should be sweet or heavy. It is a beer for long conversations.</p>",
